.TH ILOGB 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
ilogb, ilogbf, ilogbl, logb, logbf, logbl \- extract unbiased exponent of a floating-point number
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "int ilogb(double " x );
.br
.BI "int ilogbf(float " x );
.br
.BI "int ilogbl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double logb(double " x );
.br
.BI "float logbf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double logbl(long double " x );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR ilogb ()
and
.BR logb ()
families return the exponent part of their argument
.IR x ,
that is, the unbiased integer power of
.B FLT_RADIX
(2 on Substrate and other IEC 60559 / IEEE\ 754 binary platforms) such
that
.PP
.RS 4
.IR FLT_RADIX ^ result
" <= |" x "| < " FLT_RADIX ^( result +1)
.RE
.PP
for finite, non-zero
.IR x .
The result is the value
.IR k
that
.BR frexp (3)
would compute as its exponent output, minus 1; equivalently, it is the
floor of the base-2 logarithm of the absolute value of
.IR x .
For example,
.BR ilogb (1.0)
is 0,
.BR ilogb (2.0)
is 1,
.BR ilogb (0.5)
is \-1, and
.BR ilogb (1024.0)
is 10.
.PP
The
.BR ilogb ()
function returns the exponent as an
.B int
suitable for passing to
.BR scalbn (3).
The
.BR logb ()
function returns the same exponent value, but typed as a floating-point
number.  The two interfaces are intended to be paired with
.BR scalbn (3)
and
.BR scalb (3)
(or
.BR ldexp (3))
respectively to perform efficient multiplication and division by powers
of the floating-point radix.
.PP
The
.B f
suffixed variants
.RB ( ilogbf (),
.BR logbf ())
take
.B float
arguments; the
.B l
suffixed variants
.RB ( ilogbl (),
.BR logbl ())
take
.B "long double"
arguments.
.PP
For a subnormal argument,
.BR logb ()
and
.BR ilogb ()
return the exponent that
.I x
would have if it were normalized; the value is therefore less than
.B \-1022
on platforms where
.B double
is IEEE\ 754 binary64.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR ilogb ()
and
.BR logb ()
return the unbiased exponent of
.IR x .
.PP
The following special cases apply to
.BR ilogb ():
.TP
.B "ilogb(0.0)"
returns
.B FP_ILOGB0
and a domain error occurs.
.TP
.B "ilogb(\(+-Inf)"
returns
.B INT_MAX
and a domain error occurs.
.TP
.B "ilogb(NaN)"
returns
.B FP_ILOGBNAN
and a domain error occurs.
.PP
The following special cases apply to
.BR logb ():
.TP
.B "logb(\(+-0)"
returns
.B \-HUGE_VAL
(that is, negative infinity), and a pole error occurs.
.TP
.B "logb(\(+-Inf)"
returns
.BR +HUGE_VAL .
.TP
.B "logb(NaN)"
returns a NaN.
.PP
The
.B <math.h>
header defines the integer constants
.B FP_ILOGB0
and
.BR FP_ILOGBNAN ,
which are the values returned by
.BR ilogb ()
for an argument of zero and NaN, respectively.  On Substrate these
expand to
.B INT_MIN
and
.BR INT_MAX ;
portable code must not assume specific values and should instead test
the argument for zero or NaN before calling
.BR ilogb ().
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur for
.BR ilogb ():
.TP
Domain error: \fIx\fP is 0, \(+-infinity, or NaN
An invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.B EDOM
(the C standard permits implementations to use
.B ERANGE
instead for the zero case; Substrate uses
.BR EDOM ).
.PP
The following errors can occur for
.BR logb ():
.TP
Pole error: \fIx\fP is 0
A divide-by-zero floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_DIVBYZERO )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.PP
.BR logb ()
does not raise a domain error or set
.I errno
for
.B \(+-Inf
or NaN arguments; their handling follows ordinary IEC\ 60559 propagation
rules.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR ilogb (),
.BR ilogbf (),
.BR ilogbl (),
.BR logb (),
.BR logbf (),
.BR logbl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR ilogb (),
.BR logb (),
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The handling of zero,
infinity, and NaN follows C99 Annex F.10.3.6 and F.10.3.10
(IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program extracts the unbiased exponent of
.B 1024.0
with both
.BR ilogb ()
and
.BR logb ()
to illustrate the difference in return type, and shows the special
return values for zero, infinity, and NaN:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <errno.h>
#include <fenv.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    int    e_i;
    double e_d;

    /* Ordinary case: 1024.0 == 2^10. */
    e_i = ilogb(1024.0);
    e_d = logb(1024.0);
    printf("ilogb(1024.0) = %d (int)\en",    e_i);
    printf("logb (1024.0) = %.1f (double)\en", e_d);

    /* Half: 0.5 == 2^-1. */
    printf("ilogb(0.5)    = %d\en", ilogb(0.5));

    /* Zero: ilogb returns FP_ILOGB0; logb returns -inf. */
    feclearexcept(FE_ALL_EXCEPT);
    errno = 0;
    e_i = ilogb(0.0);
    printf("ilogb(0.0)    = %d (FP_ILOGB0=%d, errno=%d)\en",
           e_i, FP_ILOGB0, errno);

    feclearexcept(FE_ALL_EXCEPT);
    errno = 0;
    e_d = logb(0.0);
    printf("logb (0.0)    = %.1f (errno=%d)\en", e_d, errno);

    /* NaN: ilogb returns FP_ILOGBNAN; logb returns NaN. */
    printf("ilogb(NaN)    = %d (FP_ILOGBNAN=%d)\en",
           ilogb(NAN), FP_ILOGBNAN);

    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A representative output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ilogb(1024.0) = 10 (int)
logb (1024.0) = 10.0 (double)
ilogb(0.5)    = -1
ilogb(0.0)    = -2147483648 (FP_ILOGB0=-2147483648, errno=33)
logb (0.0)    = -inf (errno=34)
ilogb(NaN)    = 2147483647 (FP_ILOGBNAN=2147483647)
.EE
.RE
.PP
The exponent returned by
.BR ilogb ()
is the value
.I n
that satisfies
.BI scalbn(x ", \-" n )
in
.BR [1.0,\ 2.0) ,
making the pair the natural building blocks for radix-2 normalization.
